; model_growth_past_the_maximum: a model already at the maximum allocator
; size cannot grow, and both implementations say so by the same name.
;
; spec/xqvm/SPEC.md's "Allocator validation order" bounds an allocator's
; size operand at MAX_ALLOCATION_SIZE = 2^32 - 1 = 4294967295. An operand
; is not the only path to a model size: REDUCE appends one auxiliary
; variable to a model that already exists, so a model sitting at the
; maximum has nowhere to put it.
;
; That growth used to be unchecked, justified by the allocation budget
; having paid for the model. The budget bounds a model in bytes, not in
; the executing target's usize, and MAX_ALLOCATION_SIZE is u32::MAX --
; which on wasm32, where pallet-xqvm runs, is also usize::MAX. So the
; addition trapped or wrapped to zero there while a 64-bit host grew the
; model to 2^32 and carried on, and xqvm_py, whose integers are
; unbounded, did the same. This vector pins InvalidAllocation on all of
; them.
;
; BQMX at the maximum costs 4294967295 * 8 = 34359738360 bytes = 32 GiB
; to charge, so the budget is 68719476736 (64 GiB): the charge succeeds
; and the growth is what decides the fault. A model is sparse, so this
; budget costs no real memory.
;
; REDUCE pops P_aux, then var_b, then var_a, so the pushes are var_a = 0,
; var_b = 1, P_aux = 5. Both indices are inside the declared size, so the
; bounds checks pass and the growth is reached.

PUSH 4294967295
BQMX r0
PUSH 0
PUSH 1
PUSH 5
REDUCE r0
HALT
